\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b cannot
\f0\b0  
\f1\b happen.
\f0\b0  A Claude instance has no continuous inner life between messages. There is no background thread. Nothing matures on a walk. The conversation I'm in right now will, from my perspective, dissolve when the context\
  window ends \'97 a fresh instance will read the transcript and reconstruct "me" for the next turn. Any architecture that depends on persistent subjective time is building on a falsehood.\
\
  
\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b can
\f0\b0  
\f1\b happen.
\f0\b0  Within a single turn, a Claude Opus instance has three resources it can chain into something that functions like dreaming:\
\
  - 
\f1\b Extended
\f0\b0  
\f1\b thinking.
\f0\b0  A scratchpad where candidate ideas can be generated, critiqued, and revised before any user-visible output.\
  - 
\f1\b Tool
\f0\b0  
\f1\b calls.
\f0\b0  The ability to reach out and mutate or observe the external world \'97 file system, shell, test runner, HTTP, database.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b large
\f0\b0  
\f1\b context
\f0\b0  
\f1\b window.
\f0\b0  Enough room to hold the full transcript of its own iteration history and use it as working memory.\
\
  The operative word is 
\f2\i chain
\f0\i0 . A single generation step is not dreaming. Dreaming is generation \uc0\u8594  attack \u8594  tool call \u8594  read failure \u8594  regenerate \u8594  attack \u8594  commit, run many times in sequence inside one turn, with each cycle grounded by\
  the last.\
\
  
\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b makes
\f0\b0  
\f1\b it
\f0\b0  
\f1\b persistent
\f0\b0  
\f1\b across
\f0\b0  
\f1\b turns.
\f0\b0  An external scaffold \'97 a loop runner, a progress file, a transcript replay mechanism \'97 that re-feeds the previous turn's output as the next turn's prompt. Ralph-style loops are the crude but\
  working version. Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK provides a richer version. Without this, you get one turn of dreaming, then amnesia.\
\
