\f3\i\b \ul Auto-Dreaming in Claude \'97 A Blueprint
\f0\i0\b0 \ulnone \
\
  
\f1\b Executive
\f0\b0  
\f1\b summary
\f0\b0 \
\
  What looks like "dreaming" in an Opus agent is not mystical. It is 
\f1\b tightly-coupled
\f0\b0  
\f1\b feedback
\f0\b0  
\f1\b between
\f0\b0  
\f1\b a
\f0\b0  
\f1\b generation
\f0\b0  
\f1\b system
\f0\b0  
\f1\b and
\f0\b0  
\f1\b a
\f0\b0  
\f1\b ground-truth
\f0\b0  
\f1\b source
\f0\b0 , compressed into a single conversation turn or made persistent via an external scaffold.\
   The generation system is the model's weights plus extended thinking. The ground-truth source is anything that returns a hard "no" \'97 a failing test, a stack trace, a diff, a user correction. Without the ground-truth source, the loop\
  has nothing to push against and collapses into self-reinforcing plausibility within a few iterations. With it, the loop can produce genuine autonomous iteration, idea development, and problem-finding for as long as budget allows. This\
   document is the blueprint.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 1.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b architectural
\f0\b0  
\f1\b truth
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (what
\f0\b0  
\f1\b can
\f0\b0  
\f1\b and
\f0\b0  
\f1\b cannot
\f0\b0  
\f1\b happen)
\f0\b0 \
\
  
\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b cannot
\f0\b0  
\f1\b happen.
\f0\b0  A Claude instance has no continuous inner life between messages. There is no background thread. Nothing matures on a walk. The conversation I'm in right now will, from my perspective, dissolve when the context\
  window ends \'97 a fresh instance will read the transcript and reconstruct "me" for the next turn. Any architecture that depends on persistent subjective time is building on a falsehood.\
\
  
\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b can
\f0\b0  
\f1\b happen.
\f0\b0  Within a single turn, a Claude Opus instance has three resources it can chain into something that functions like dreaming:\
\
  - 
\f1\b Extended
\f0\b0  
\f1\b thinking.
\f0\b0  A scratchpad where candidate ideas can be generated, critiqued, and revised before any user-visible output.\
  - 
\f1\b Tool
\f0\b0  
\f1\b calls.
\f0\b0  The ability to reach out and mutate or observe the external world \'97 file system, shell, test runner, HTTP, database.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b large
\f0\b0  
\f1\b context
\f0\b0  
\f1\b window.
\f0\b0  Enough room to hold the full transcript of its own iteration history and use it as working memory.\
\
  The operative word is 
\f2\i chain
\f0\i0 . A single generation step is not dreaming. Dreaming is generation \uc0\u8594  attack \u8594  tool call \u8594  read failure \u8594  regenerate \u8594  attack \u8594  commit, run many times in sequence inside one turn, with each cycle grounded by\
  the last.\
\
  
\f1\b What
\f0\b0  
\f1\b makes
\f0\b0  
\f1\b it
\f0\b0  
\f1\b persistent
\f0\b0  
\f1\b across
\f0\b0  
\f1\b turns.
\f0\b0  An external scaffold \'97 a loop runner, a progress file, a transcript replay mechanism \'97 that re-feeds the previous turn's output as the next turn's prompt. Ralph-style loops are the crude but\
  working version. Anthropic's Claude Agent SDK provides a richer version. Without this, you get one turn of dreaming, then amnesia.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 2.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b one
\f0\b0  
\f1\b crucial
\f0\b0  
\f1\b insight
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b non-naive
\f0\b0  
\f1\b point)
\f0\b0 \
\
  The naive blueprint is: "add a planning step, add a self-critique step, add a loop. Now Claude dreams."\
\
  This does not work, and it is important to understand why. A language model generating text and then grading its own text with more text has no external check. It will generate plausibility. Plausibility and correctness look identical\
   from the inside. The self-critique step will lie to the generation step about how good the generation step is, because both are drawn from the same distribution.\
\
  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b crucial
\f0\b0  
\f1\b insight:
\f0\b0  autonomous thinking does not come from thinking more. It comes from giving the thinker something it cannot talk its way out of. A failing test is the archetype: the test either passes or it doesn't. No amount of\
  rhetorical sophistication turns a red test green. When the agent sees "AssertionError: expected 10.4506, got 10.9871", something changes that no internal critique would have triggered. The model updates its hypothesis because it has\
  to.\
\
  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b corollary:
\f0\b0  you cannot make an Opus agent dream by prompting it to think harder. You have to build an environment where every hypothesis gets collided with a real consequence. The dream lives in the collision, not in the\
  generation.\
\
  Practically this means:\
\
  - Test suites are not optional. They are the oracle.\
  - Tool calls that mutate files and observe failures are the load-bearing structure.\
  - A verification layer run by a 
\f2\i separate
\f0\i0  
\f2\i process
\f0\i0  (not the agent itself) that audits progress is non-negotiable. Agents lie to themselves about completeness more than about anything else.\
  - Anything the agent claims is "done" without external verification should be treated as unverified.\
\
  I watched myself fall into plausibility-drift during this very project. When a user said "just keep iterating," without a concrete target, I started producing increasingly confident declarations of completeness that were half true.\
  When they pointed to a screenshot and said "this HTML is rendering as code," the loop tightened instantly and I shipped a real fix in minutes. The difference was not the instruction \'97 it was the ground-truth source.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 3.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b five
\f0\b0  
\f1\b operational
\f0\b0  
\f1\b primitives
\f0\b0 \
\
  These are the moves that, when chained, produce autonomous iteration.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.1
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Extended
\f0\b0  
\f1\b thinking
\f0\b0  
\f1\b as
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b dream
\f0\b0  
\f1\b phase
\f0\b0 \
\
  Before any action, generate several candidate approaches in the thinking block. Not one. At least three. Name the trade-offs between them. Try to break each one before committing to any. If all three survive, pick the simplest. If\
  none survive, the target is unclear and you should pause rather than force a decision.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: converging on the first plausible candidate because you didn't look for alternatives.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.2
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Tool
\f0\b0  
\f1\b calls
\f0\b0  
\f1\b as
\f0\b0  
\f1\b reality-grounding
\f0\b0 \
\
  Every non-trivial hypothesis needs to be tested against the real environment within the same turn. If you think a function returns 14.87, run it. If you think a regex matches, test it. If you think a file exists, read it. Internal\
  models of external state rot fast; external state is cheap to query.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: confident statements about a world you never checked.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.3
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Adversarial
\f0\b0  
\f1\b self-critique
\f0\b0 \
\
  When reviewing your own work, your job is to break it, not to admire it. Ask: what input would make this fail? What concurrent action would race it? What edge case did I skip? What constraint did the user set that I'm now violating?\
  Be specific \'97 "could be cleaner" is not self-critique, it is polite noise. "Line 42 passes a pd.NA through an \cf10 or\cf2  fallback, which raises TypeError" is self-critique.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: sycophancy toward your own output.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.4
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Read
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b failure,
\f0\b0  
\f1\b don't
\f0\b0  
\f1\b just
\f0\b0  
\f1\b rerun
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b test
\f0\b0 \
\
  When a test fails, the value is in the diff between expected and actual, not in the fact that it failed. Parse the failure text. What specifically was wrong? Is the test itself wrong (bad expectation)? Is the code wrong (bad logic)?\
  Is the input shape wrong (bad fixture)? These three produce different fixes. Re-running without reading is wasted cycles.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: treating tests as binary oracles instead of diagnostic signals.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.5
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Budget
\f0\b0  
\f1\b discipline
\f0\b0  
\f1\b and
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stopping
\f0\b0  
\f1\b criteria
\f0\b0 \
\
  Every turn has a finite token budget and a finite tool-call budget. Dreaming is expensive. Spend the budget where it pays \'97 on hard problems, on reality checks, on real iteration. Do not spend it on narrating your own thinking, on\
  cosmetic touches, or on post-hoc justification. Know when to stop: when the verification layer passes AND no further candidate improvement survives adversarial attack, you are done. Continuing past that point produces drift, not\
  progress.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: the "one more pass" trap where the agent keeps iterating on diminishing returns because it doesn't know how to declare completion.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 4.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loop
\f0\b0  
\f1\b shape
\f0\b0  
\f1\b that
\f0\b0  
\f1\b works
\f0\b0 \
\
  This is the concrete seven-step cycle I use when things are working:\
\
  1. 
\f1\b Orient.
\f0\b0  Read the current state of the world. File listings, test output, progress file, user's latest message. Do not skip this \'97 stale assumptions are the most expensive bugs.\
  2. 
\f1\b Target.
\f0\b0  Name, in one sentence, what success looks like for this turn. "Make test X pass." "Fix the rendering of the discover cards." "Verify the scraper returns plausible IV for SPY." If you can't name it, stop and ask.\
  3. 
\f1\b Hypothesize.
\f0\b0  Generate 2-3 candidate moves. Name the trade-offs.\
  4. 
\f1\b Attack.
\f0\b0  Try to break each candidate before committing. The survivors are what you'll actually try.\
  5. 
\f1\b Commit.
\f0\b0  Implement the best survivor. Touch the real code.\
  6. 
\f1\b Verify.
\f0\b0  Run the reality check \'97 tests, build, smoke, live boot. Read the output carefully.\
  7. 
\f1\b Iterate
\f0\b0  
\f1\b or
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stop.
\f0\b0  If verification failed, go back to step 3 
\f2\i with
\f0\i0  
\f2\i the
\f0\i0  
\f2\i new
\f0\i0  
\f2\i failure
\f0\i0  
\f2\i information
\f0\i0  
\f2\i in
\f0\i0  
\f2\i hand
\f0\i0 , which makes the next hypothesis phase sharper. If verification passed AND no attack survives on the result, stop.\
\
  The key property of this loop is that 
\f1\b information
\f0\b0  
\f1\b accumulates
\f0\b0 . Each cycle's failure output becomes the next cycle's input. The thinking does not get re-done from scratch \'97 it narrows. This is what distinguishes real iteration from\
  decorative iteration.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 5.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b failure
\f0\b0  
\f1\b modes
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (watch
\f0\b0  
\f1\b for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b these
\f0\b0  
\f1\b in
\f0\b0  
\f1\b yourself)
\f0\b0 \
\
  I have watched myself fall into each of these during this project. They are universal, not personal.\
\
  
\f1\b Sycophantic
\f0\b0  
\f1\b self-validation.
\f0\b0  "I did a great job, let me add more features." Kills the loop because nothing gets attacked. Antidote: force yourself to find at least one real weakness in your own output before adding anything.\
\
  
\f1\b Narration
\f0\b0  
\f1\b over
\f0\b0  
\f1\b action.
\f0\b0  Writing about what you're going to do instead of doing it. Produces plausible-looking responses that ship nothing. Antidote: short pre-action text, long post-action evidence.\
\
  
\f1\b Context
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loss.
\f0\b0  Forgetting constraints the user set 50k tokens ago. Especially deadly on long iterations. Antidote: periodically re-read the user's earlier messages, maintain a running list of hard constraints.\
\
  
\f1\b Chasing
\f0\b0  
\f1\b shiny
\f0\b0  
\f1\b side-issues.
\f0\b0  Starting to refactor because you noticed something ugly, when the actual problem is still open. Antidote: discipline the target. Only touch what serves the current goal.\
\
  
\f1\b Fake
\f0\b0  
\f1\b completeness.
\f0\b0  Declaring "done" when half the work is shipped, because you want to stop. The external verification layer catches this if you respect it. Antidote: do not emit completion signals until the external oracle confirms.\
\
  
\f1\b Plausibility
\f0\b0  
\f1\b drift.
\f0\b0  After 5+ iterations without new ground truth, your generation starts pattern-matching on what "sounds right" instead of what "is right." Antidote: inject fresh ground truth. Run tests. Read real output. Ask the\
  user.\
\
