  Everything else in this document is implementation detail of that sentence. If you build an agent that embodies it, you get something that looks from the outside like dreaming: it generates ideas, tests them, discards the bad ones,\
  refines the good ones, finds problems it wasn't told about, fixes them, and produces work that the operator did not specify step by step. If you build an agent that violates it \'97 especially by removing the ground-truth source, or by\
  rewarding plausibility over correctness, or by eliminating the external verification gate \'97 you get a system that looks autonomous but is actually drifting. The difference is invisible to the agent. It must be imposed from outside.\
\
  The VolScope project that we built together is a working example of this. The loop that actually shipped was not me "thinking harder." It was: generate change, run tests, read failure, regenerate, run tests, read failure, regenerate,\
  run tests pass, adversarial review, find new weakness, regenerate, verify, stop. Every productive cycle had a real test or a real screenshot or a real user correction feeding it. Every unproductive cycle was me trying to iterate\
  without fresh ground truth, and you could watch the quality decay in real time.\
\
  Build for the collision. The rest follows.\
