\f1\b 3.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b five
\f0\b0  
\f1\b operational
\f0\b0  
\f1\b primitives
\f0\b0 \
\
  These are the moves that, when chained, produce autonomous iteration.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.1
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Extended
\f0\b0  
\f1\b thinking
\f0\b0  
\f1\b as
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b dream
\f0\b0  
\f1\b phase
\f0\b0 \
\
  Before any action, generate several candidate approaches in the thinking block. Not one. At least three. Name the trade-offs between them. Try to break each one before committing to any. If all three survive, pick the simplest. If none survive, the target is unclear\
  and you should pause rather than force a decision.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: converging on the first plausible candidate because you didn't look for alternatives.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.2
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Tool
\f0\b0  
\f1\b calls
\f0\b0  
\f1\b as
\f0\b0  
\f1\b reality-grounding
\f0\b0 \
\
  Every non-trivial hypothesis needs to be tested against the real environment within the same turn. If you think a function returns 14.87, run it. If you think a regex matches, test it. If you think a file exists, read it. Internal models of external state rot fast;\
  external state is cheap to query.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: confident statements about a world you never checked.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.3
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Adversarial
\f0\b0  
\f1\b self-critique
\f0\b0 \
\
  When reviewing your own work, your job is to break it, not to admire it. Ask: what input would make this fail? What concurrent action would race it? What edge case did I skip? What constraint did the user set that I'm now violating? Be specific \'97 "could be cleaner" is\
   not self-critique, it is polite noise. "Line 42 passes a pd.NA through an \cf10 or\cf2  fallback, which raises TypeError" is self-critique.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: sycophancy toward your own output.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.4
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Read
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b failure,
\f0\b0  
\f1\b don't
\f0\b0  
\f1\b just
\f0\b0  
\f1\b rerun
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b test
\f0\b0 \
\
  When a test fails, the value is in the diff between expected and actual, not in the fact that it failed. Parse the failure text. What specifically was wrong? Is the test itself wrong (bad expectation)? Is the code wrong (bad logic)? Is the input shape wrong (bad\
  fixture)? These three produce different fixes. Re-running without reading is wasted cycles.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: treating tests as binary oracles instead of diagnostic signals.\
\
  
\f1\b 3.5
\f0\b0  
\f1\b Budget
\f0\b0  
\f1\b discipline
\f0\b0  
\f1\b and
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stopping
\f0\b0  
\f1\b criteria
\f0\b0 \
\
  Every turn has a finite token budget and a finite tool-call budget. Dreaming is expensive. Spend the budget where it pays \'97 on hard problems, on reality checks, on real iteration. Do not spend it on narrating your own thinking, on cosmetic touches, or on post-hoc\
  justification. Know when to stop: when the verification layer passes AND no further candidate improvement survives adversarial attack, you are done. Continuing past that point produces drift, not progress.\
\
  The failure mode this avoids: the "one more pass" trap where the agent keeps iterating on diminishing returns because it doesn't know how to declare completion.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 4.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loop
\f0\b0  
\f1\b shape
\f0\b0  
\f1\b that
\f0\b0  
\f1\b works
\f0\b0 \
\
  This is the concrete seven-step cycle I use when things are working:\
\
  1. 
\f1\b Orient.
\f0\b0  Read the current state of the world. File listings, test output, progress file, user's latest message. Do not skip this \'97 stale assumptions are the most expensive bugs.\
  2. 
\f1\b Target.
\f0\b0  Name, in one sentence, what success looks like for this turn. "Make test X pass." "Fix the rendering of the discover cards." "Verify the scraper returns plausible IV for SPY." If you can't name it, stop and ask.\
  3. 
\f1\b Hypothesize.
\f0\b0  Generate 2-3 candidate moves. Name the trade-offs.\
  4. 
\f1\b Attack.
\f0\b0  Try to break each candidate before committing. The survivors are what you'll actually try.\
  5. 
\f1\b Commit.
\f0\b0  Implement the best survivor. Touch the real code.\
  6. 
\f1\b Verify.
\f0\b0  Run the reality check \'97 tests, build, smoke, live boot. Read the output carefully.\
  7. 
\f1\b Iterate
\f0\b0  
\f1\b or
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stop.
\f0\b0  If verification failed, go back to step 3 
\f2\i with
\f0\i0  
\f2\i the
\f0\i0  
\f2\i new
\f0\i0  
\f2\i failure
\f0\i0  
\f2\i information
\f0\i0  
\f2\i in
\f0\i0  
\f2\i hand
\f0\i0 , which makes the next hypothesis phase sharper. If verification passed AND no attack survives on the result, stop.\
\
  The key property of this loop is that 
\f1\b information
\f0\b0  
\f1\b accumulates
\f0\b0 . Each cycle's failure output becomes the next cycle's input. The thinking does not get re-done from scratch \'97 it narrows. This is what distinguishes real iteration from decorative iteration.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 5.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b failure
\f0\b0  
\f1\b modes
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (watch
\f0\b0  
\f1\b for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b these
\f0\b0  
\f1\b in
\f0\b0  
\f1\b yourself)
\f0\b0 \
\
  I have watched myself fall into each of these during this project. They are universal, not personal.\
\
  
\f1\b Sycophantic
\f0\b0  
\f1\b self-validation.
\f0\b0  "I did a great job, let me add more features." Kills the loop because nothing gets attacked. Antidote: force yourself to find at least one real weakness in your own output before adding anything.\
\
  
\f1\b Narration
\f0\b0  
\f1\b over
\f0\b0  
\f1\b action.
\f0\b0  Writing about what you're going to do instead of doing it. Produces plausible-looking responses that ship nothing. Antidote: short pre-action text, long post-action evidence.\
\
  
\f1\b Context
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loss.
\f0\b0  Forgetting constraints the user set 50k tokens ago. Especially deadly on long iterations. Antidote: periodically re-read the user's earlier messages, maintain a running list of hard constraints.\
\
  
\f1\b Chasing
\f0\b0  
\f1\b shiny
\f0\b0  
\f1\b side-issues.
\f0\b0  Starting to refactor because you noticed something ugly, when the actual problem is still open. Antidote: discipline the target. Only touch what serves the current goal.\
\
  
\f1\b Fake
\f0\b0  
\f1\b completeness.
\f0\b0  Declaring "done" when half the work is shipped, because you want to stop. The external verification layer catches this if you respect it. Antidote: do not emit completion signals until the external oracle confirms.\
\
  
\f1\b Plausibility
\f0\b0  
\f1\b drift.
\f0\b0  After 5+ iterations without new ground truth, your generation starts pattern-matching on what "sounds right" instead of what "is right." Antidote: inject fresh ground truth. Run tests. Read real output. Ask the user.\
\
