\f0\b0  
\f1\b persistence)
\f0\b0 \
\
  An Opus agent inside a single turn can dream for one session. To sustain dreaming across days you need scaffolding 
\f2\i outside
\f0\i0  the model:\
\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loop
\f0\b0  
\f1\b runner
\f0\b0  that captures the agent's output and re-feeds it as the next turn's prompt. Ralph loops are the minimal version. Anthropic's Agent SDK is the richer version.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b progress
\f0\b0  
\f1\b file
\f0\b0  the agent maintains across turns \'97 JSON or markdown, summarizing what's done, what's open, what's blocked. This compresses the effective memory from "entire transcript" to "relevant state."\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b verification
\f0\b0  
\f1\b process
\f0\b0  
\f1\b run
\f0\b0  
\f1\b separately
\f0\b0  
\f1\b from
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b agent
\f0\b0  that audits the progress file against reality. If the file says "T17 done," the verification process runs T17's tests and confirms. The agent should not grade itself.\
  - 
\f1\b Hard
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stopping
\f0\b0  
\f1\b criteria.
\f0\b0  Max iterations, completion promise requiring verification to pass, time budget. Agents that can self-terminate without external limits tend to either loop forever or terminate prematurely.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b memory
\f0\b0  
\f1\b system
\f0\b0  
\f1\b for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b facts
\f0\b0  
\f1\b that
\f0\b0  
\f1\b persist.
\f0\b0  In Claude Code this is the auto-memory directory. Durable notes about the user, their preferences, known gotchas. Not a substitute for the transcript, but a way to stop re-learning the same\
  things.\
\
  Without this scaffold, a multi-turn session is just single-turn sessions in a trench coat. With it, the feedback loop extends across days and can genuinely develop ideas that single turns cannot.\
\
