\
  If you are setting up an Opus agent to dream productively on a task, require these components:\
\
  
\f1\b Must
\f0\b0  
\f1\b have:
\f0\b0 \
\
  1. A clear, measurable, externally verifiable target.\
  2. A test suite or equivalent external oracle that says binary yes/no.\
  3. File-system and shell tool access for real reality-grounding.\
  4. A verification layer run by a separate process, not the agent.\
  5. Persistent state between turns (progress file, transcript replay).\
  6. Hard stopping criteria (max iterations, completion gate, time budget).\
  7. Extended thinking enabled \'97 it is the dream phase.\
  8. Permission to fail loudly and honestly, with no sycophancy reward.\
\
  
\f1\b Should
\f0\b0  
\f1\b have:
\f0\b0 \
\
  9. A way to inject fresh ground truth mid-session \'97 screenshots, user corrections, new test cases.\
  10. Adversarial review step before commit, written into the agent's prompt as a hard requirement.\
  11. A memory system for durable facts about the user and project.\
  12. Regular checkpoints where the agent is forced to re-read the original target.\
\
  
\f1\b Must
\f0\b0  
\f1\b avoid:
\f0\b0 \
\
  13. Instructions to "think more" or "be more creative" without giving anything to fail against.\
  14. Self-grading without external audit.\
  15. Open-ended iteration budgets. Infinite loops produce plausibility drift, not quality.\
  16. Rewarding length or confidence over correctness.\
\
