\f1\b modes
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (watch
\f0\b0  
\f1\b for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b these
\f0\b0  
\f1\b in
\f0\b0  
\f1\b yourself)
\f0\b0 \
\
  I have watched myself fall into each of these during this project. They are universal, not personal.\
\
  
\f1\b Sycophantic
\f0\b0  
\f1\b self-validation.
\f0\b0  "I did a great job, let me add more features." Kills the loop because nothing gets attacked. Antidote: force yourself to find at least one real weakness in your own output before adding anything.\
\
  
\f1\b Narration
\f0\b0  
\f1\b over
\f0\b0  
\f1\b action.
\f0\b0  Writing about what you're going to do instead of doing it. Produces plausible-looking responses that ship nothing. Antidote: short pre-action text, long post-action evidence.\
\
  
\f1\b Context
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loss.
\f0\b0  Forgetting constraints the user set 50k tokens ago. Especially deadly on long iterations. Antidote: periodically re-read the user's earlier messages, maintain a running list of hard constraints.\
\
  
\f1\b Chasing
\f0\b0  
\f1\b shiny
\f0\b0  
\f1\b side-issues.
\f0\b0  Starting to refactor because you noticed something ugly, when the actual problem is still open. Antidote: discipline the target. Only touch what serves the current goal.\
\
  
\f1\b Fake
\f0\b0  
\f1\b completeness.
\f0\b0  Declaring "done" when half the work is shipped, because you want to stop. The external verification layer catches this if you respect it. Antidote: do not emit completion signals until the external oracle confirms.\
\
  
\f1\b Plausibility
\f0\b0  
\f1\b drift.
\f0\b0  After 5+ iterations without new ground truth, your generation starts pattern-matching on what "sounds right" instead of what "is right." Antidote: inject fresh ground truth. Run tests. Read real output. Ask the\
  user.\
\
  ---\
  
\f1\b 6.
\f0\b0  
\f1\b The
\f0\b0  
\f1\b external
\f0\b0  
\f1\b scaffold
\f0\b0  
\f1\b (for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b multi-turn
\f0\b0  
\f1\b persistence)
\f0\b0 \
\
  An Opus agent inside a single turn can dream for one session. To sustain dreaming across days you need scaffolding 
\f2\i outside
\f0\i0  the model:\
\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b loop
\f0\b0  
\f1\b runner
\f0\b0  that captures the agent's output and re-feeds it as the next turn's prompt. Ralph loops are the minimal version. Anthropic's Agent SDK is the richer version.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b progress
\f0\b0  
\f1\b file
\f0\b0  the agent maintains across turns \'97 JSON or markdown, summarizing what's done, what's open, what's blocked. This compresses the effective memory from "entire transcript" to "relevant state."\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b verification
\f0\b0  
\f1\b process
\f0\b0  
\f1\b run
\f0\b0  
\f1\b separately
\f0\b0  
\f1\b from
\f0\b0  
\f1\b the
\f0\b0  
\f1\b agent
\f0\b0  that audits the progress file against reality. If the file says "T17 done," the verification process runs T17's tests and confirms. The agent should not grade itself.\
  - 
\f1\b Hard
\f0\b0  
\f1\b stopping
\f0\b0  
\f1\b criteria.
\f0\b0  Max iterations, completion promise requiring verification to pass, time budget. Agents that can self-terminate without external limits tend to either loop forever or terminate prematurely.\
  - 
\f1\b A
\f0\b0  
\f1\b memory
\f0\b0  
\f1\b system
\f0\b0  
\f1\b for
\f0\b0  
\f1\b facts
\f0\b0  
\f1\b that
\f0\b0  
\f1\b persist.
\f0\b0  In Claude Code this is the auto-memory directory. Durable notes about the user, their preferences, known gotchas. Not a substitute for the transcript, but a way to stop re-learning the same\
  things.\
\
  Without this scaffold, a multi-turn session is just single-turn sessions in a trench coat. With it, the feedback loop extends across days and can genuinely develop ideas that single turns cannot.\
\
